  "about": "The claim parser's word tables (kernel/speech.py; docs/design/VERIFY_HARDENING.md 13.1). Phrases are plain English and are tokenized with the parser's own tokenizer at load (contractions split: \"isn't\" -> is n't, \"I'll\" -> i will, \"can't\" -> can n't), so \"don't know if\" matches \"do not know if\" and \"don't know if\". State and issue words, synonyms and units come from the shared vocabulary (data/vocab/*.json); this file holds the function words: negation, hedges and modality, questions, commitments, directives, refusals, attribution, quantifiers, numbers (number_singles compose with number_scales / number_totals: \"two hundred and fifty\") and times, spoken unit spellings (unit_aliases), sentence adverbs that split a clause only before a new subject, hedge or condition (parenthetical_clause_words), denial and void frames and retractions (denial_prefix / denial_suffix / void_prefix / retractions; self_retractions, the ones that can only mean the speaker's own words, also take back the speaker's previous utterance), lifted directives (permission_words with a permission_head and a negator: \"you don't need to\"), the conditions that wait for a hazard to be gone (safe_condition_markers + safe_condition_words), location claims (location_claim_preps, location_adverbs) and placement destinations (placement_acts, destination_preps). Matching is lexical and deterministic and never reads hidden state; a statement outside these tables asserts nothing (section 26).",
